MERIDIAN — Historical Intelligence

Dossier: Research / Investigation
ID: 04
Status: Foundation

---

Historical intelligence in MERIDIAN begins with a simple distinction:

A historical building is not the same thing as the image of a historical building.

The project is interested in what can be learned from architecture before deciding what can be transformed from it.

This means history is approached as a field of relationships rather than a catalogue of recognizable forms.

---

Reading the historical source

A historical work may be examined through several interconnected layers:

Form — geometry, massing, proportion, repetition, rhythm, profile

Space — organization, sequence, centre, boundary, void, threshold

Order — hierarchy, symmetry, axis, modular relationships, directional systems

Construction — structure, material, assembly, load, tectonic expression

Experience — movement, view, compression, release, light, shadow, perception

Ornament — pattern, density, surface, repetition, abstraction

Condition — climate, landscape, ritual, social structure, technology, available materials

These are not separate boxes.

A dome, for example, may simultaneously be geometric, structural, spatial, environmental, symbolic, and perceptual.

The research must therefore preserve relationships between layers rather than reducing a building to a list of characteristics.

---

From object to intelligence

The first question is:

What is actually there?

The second is:

How does it work?

Only after these questions can another one be asked:

What could be transformed?

This creates an important distinction:

"Historical Object → Historical Logic → Architectural Operation"

The object belongs to history.

The logic is an interpretation of relationships within that object.

The operation is what MERIDIAN may eventually do with that interpretation.

These three should never be silently merged.

---

The danger of resemblance

A transformed building may resemble its historical source and still have no meaningful transformation behind it.

The opposite is also possible: a result may look unfamiliar while retaining a strong relationship to the logic from which it was derived.

Visual distance is therefore not a reliable measure of transformation.

Nor is visual similarity reliable evidence of continuity.

The research must be able to explain the relationship rather than rely on appearance to make the argument.

---

Comparative intelligence

Historical sources become more useful when they can be placed beside one another.

The purpose of comparison is not to rank civilizations or search for a single universal architectural principle.

It is to notice:

- recurring relationships
- meaningful differences
- different solutions to similar problems
- similar operations appearing in different conditions
- contradictions between interpretations
- relationships that disappear when architecture is reduced to style

Comparison should therefore produce questions, not just classifications.

---

The four initial fields

MERIDIAN currently approaches historical knowledge through four broad fields:

- Byzantine / Renaissance
- Russian
- Ilkhanid / Iranian
- Achaemenid / Iranian

These are research territories, not four styles intended for assembly.

They should not be treated as equal containers of predetermined characteristics.

Each field has to be investigated on its own historical terms before relationships between them are proposed.

If a meaningful connection cannot be supported, the connection should not be manufactured.

---

History is not a design library

The purpose of historical research is not to produce a larger vocabulary of things that can be copied.

Its purpose is to make the project more capable of seeing.

A historical source may ultimately contribute:

a spatial relationship,
a structural idea,
a proportional system,
a method of organizing movement,
a relationship between solid and void,
a way of producing hierarchy,
a condition of light,
or something that has not yet been identified.

It may also contribute nothing useful.

That possibility must remain open.

---

What historical intelligence should produce

A successful historical investigation should leave behind something more precise than a description.

It should produce one or more of the following:

an observation worth preserving,

a distinction that prevents a false reading,

a relationship that can be tested,

an interpretation that can be challenged,

an operation that can enter transformation,

a contradiction that requires further investigation,

or a question that changes the direction of the project.

The historical source is not the endpoint.

It is where the investigation begins.
